  "worked_solution": "A function $I(x,y)$ is an integrating factor for the differential equation\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nif the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\nI(x,y)[P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y]=0\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{3}}(x\\,\\text{d}y-y\\,\\text{d}x) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}.\n$$\n\nFor our transformed equation, we have  \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})=-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})=-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation $\\text{is}$ exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{3}}$ $\\text{is}$ an integrating factor for the initial differential equation.",
